// Problem: Reverse Words in a String
// LeetCode: 151

// Approach:
// First reverse the entire string.
// Then reverse each individual word to get the words in reverse order.

class Solution {
public:
    string reverseWords(string s) {
        string ans = "";
        int n = s.size();

        reverse(s.begin(), s.end());

        int i = 0;

        while(i < n) {
            while(i < n && s[i] == ' ') {
                i++;
            }

            if(i >= n) {
                break;
            }

            int start = i;

            while(i < n && s[i] != ' ') {
                i++;
            }

            string word = s.substr(start, i - start);
            reverse(word.begin(), word.end());

            if(ans.size() > 0) {
                ans += " ";
            }

            ans += word;
        }

        return ans;
    }
};

/*
TIME COMPLEXITY: O(n)
SPACE COMPLEXITY: O(n)

Mistakes:
1. Initially tried reversing individual words without reversing the complete string,
   which reverses characters instead of word order.
2. Used reverse() assuming it returns a string, but reverse() modifies the string in-place.
3. Confused substr() second parameter with ending index; in C++, it represents length.
4. Faced issues in tracking word starting index and length using j.
5. Did not handle multiple spaces and last word edge case.

Learning:
1. Learned that reversing the whole string + reversing each word gives reversed word order.
2. Learned reverse() syntax:
   reverse(string.begin(), string.end());
3. Learned substr() syntax:
   string.substr(starting_index, length);
4. Learned to track word boundaries using start and current index.
5. Learned how to handle extra spaces while processing strings.

Possible Optimization:
Can solve using in-place two pointer technique to reduce extra space.
*/
